= Verification — Technical =

== Specification relation ==
Verification can be represented schematically as:

<pre>
artifact/system S
specification R
verification method M
evidence E
--------------------------------
E supports that S satisfies R under assumptions A
</pre>

The epistemic content of verification depends on the completeness of R, validity of M, quality of E, and truth of A.

== Formal verification ==
For a transition system <code>M</code> and property <code>φ</code>, model checking asks whether:

<pre>
M ⊨ φ
</pre>

The result is relative to the modeled state space and semantics.

Theorem proving can establish properties of programs/specifications within a formal logic, often through invariants, Hoare logic, refinement, or dependent types.

== Testing versus proof ==
Testing samples executions.

A test can demonstrate the presence of a defect for a particular case but cannot generally quantify over all possible executions.

Formal proof can quantify over modeled cases but may abstract away implementation/environment details.

Testing and formal verification are therefore complementary rather than strict substitutes.

== Numerical verification ==
Scientific computing often distinguishes:
* code verification — equations implemented correctly;
* solution verification — numerical error/mesh/time-step effects quantified;
* validation — model correspondence to relevant empirical behavior.

Convergence studies, manufactured solutions, benchmark problems, and independent implementations are common verification tools.

== Cryptographic verification ==
Cryptographic verification establishes formal relations under cryptographic assumptions.

Examples:
* signature verifies possession/use of a private key corresponding to a public key;
* hash check verifies data equality/integrity relative to a digest;
* Merkle proof verifies inclusion under a committed root.

These do not establish semantic truth of the signed or hashed content.

== Verification provenance ==
A verification claim should itself have [[Provenance/Technical|provenance]]:
* verifier/tool version;
* artifact hash;
* specification version;
* assumptions;
* logs/proofs/test vectors;
* timestamp;
* result.

This makes verification reproducible and auditable.

